  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce final detailed technical tutorial. Need ensure complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls.\n\nNeed think structure. Could be tutorial with sections. Need maybe use PostgreSQL/MySQL examples. Need explain SQL execution pipeline: client sends query, parser, analyzer, rewriter, planner/optimizer, executor, storage engine, transaction manager, result set. Include indexes: B-tree, hash, GIN/GiST, covering, partial, composite, index-only scans, index condition pushdown, statistics, cardinality estimates. Joins: nested loop, hash, merge, join order, dynamic programming, memoization, lateral, subqueries, decorrelation, common table expressions, materialized vs inlined. Transactions: ACID, MVCC, locks, isolation levels, snapshots, write-ahead log, 2PC, deadlocks, autocommit, savepoints. Returning rows: row format, serialization, cursor, streaming, fetch size, result cache, prepared statements.\n\nNeed concrete examples: schema employees, departments, orders, order_items. Query: SELECT e.name, d.name FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.dept_id=d.id WHERE e.salary > 50000 AND d.region='EMEA'; EXPLAIN. Another: SELECT o.order_id, sum(oi.amount) FROM orders o JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id=o.order_id WHERE o.created_at >= '2024-01-01' GROUP BY o.order_id HAVING sum(oi.amount)>1000.\n\nNeed tradeoffs: indexes speed reads but slow writes, storage, maintenance; too many indexes; composite column order; equality vs range; leftmost prefix; index-only scan needs visibility map; covering index. Join tradeoffs: nested loop with index good small inner, hash good large equi-joins, merge good sorted; join order matters; optimizer may choose bad due to stats; force join order? Avoid. Subquery vs join; EXISTS vs IN; DISTINCT; GROUP BY.\n\nPerformance pitfalls: SELECT *, missing indexes, implicit type conversion, functions on indexed columns, OR across",
